run_eval/run_loop: fix contaminated results and masked harness errors; add
regression tests for the fork's actual responsibilities

Two eval-validity bugs found while auditing this fork for
CtrlCarlitos/skills#1:

- A real skill already installed under the name being evaluated could
  trigger instead of the injected candidate-description decoy, and the
  harness counted that as a pass for the candidate under test. Detected
  eagerly now (find_conflicting_installed_skill) and surfaced as a loud
  warning plus an eval_validity_warning field, not silently measured.
- Any worker exception (subprocess crash, missing `claude` CLI, timeout)
  was coerced to a plain non-trigger (False), so a broken harness and a
  genuinely non-triggering description were indistinguishable in the
  reported score. run_eval.py's aggregate_results/summarize and
  run_loop.py's split_results now report an "errored" count instead, and
  run_loop.py's all_passed early-exit no longer fires on an errored run.

Also extracts the Windows pipe-reader pump into pump_stdout_to_queue()
so it's testable without sys.platform or a real subprocess, adds a
tests/ directory covering it, the UTF-8-everywhere invariant from patch
0002, --project-root resolution from patch 0003, and validate_skill/
package_skill with spaces and non-ASCII characters in the skill's path,
and updates the SKILL.md fork note to mention these fixes.
